      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You've hit on a key nuance: while growth is the primary, verifiable economic outcome, the elevation of inequality fundamentally alters society by eroding social mobility and weakening demand among the working class. Critics argue that these are not separate issues but feedback loops—when the wealth gap widens, aggregate demand can stall, which ironically undermines the very growth the deregulation was meant to spur. Therefore, the consensus likely views the \"success\" of these policies as conditional on addressing the social consequences they inadvertently created.",
      "source": "target",
